To resolve Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) errors in Next.js when using WordPress as your backend API, you need to configure CORS headers correctly on both the server-side of Next.js and the client-side of WordPress.

Here's how you can do it:

### 1. Configure CORS in Next.js

Next.js provides built-in support for CORS through its `cors` middleware. To enable this feature, add the following code to your `pages/_app.js` or `_document.js` file:

```jsx
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

const App = ({ Component, pageProps }) => {
  const [isMounted, setIsMounted] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isMounted) return;

    // Add your custom CORS policy here
    fetch('https://your-wordpress-site.com/wp-json', {
      method: 'GET',
      headers: new Headers({
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN' // Replace with actual token
      }),
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => console.log(data))
    .catch(error => console.error('Error:', error));
